  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}\u2061x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{6}\u2061x & \\,\u21d2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{ln}^{5}\u2061x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,\u21d2\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}\u2061x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{6}\u2061x)(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)(\\frac{6{ln}^{5}\u2061x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}\u2061x-6\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}\u2061x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}\u2061x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{7}\u2061x-6I \\\\ 7I & ={ln}^{7}\u2061x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{7}\u2061x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}\u2061x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}\u2061x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}\u2061(e)-{ln}^{7}\u2061(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
